    {
      "id": "ES-C315-pecho",
      "sequence": 4870,
      "title": "el pecho — the chest you can run backwards",
      "headword": "el pecho",
      "romanization": "el pecho",
      "gloss": "the chest — Latin pectus, whose ct became ch on the same road that took noctem to noche; English keeps the Latin in pectoral",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:403ae9a51879d2fa",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is an audience, literally? (A hearing.) And what flower is a lion's tooth? (The dandelion.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la noche — took the same road out of Latin."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la espalda — the other side of this one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "PE-cho, two beats. The ch is the ch of English church. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el pecho — \"the chest.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin pectus. The thing to watch is the middle of it: a Latin ct comes out as a Spanish ch, and it does so with almost no exceptions."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have already met the result without being handed the rule. Latin noctem is la noche. Latin octo is ocho. Latin factum is hecho. Latin lactem is leche. And Latin pectus is el pecho."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So when a Spanish word has ch in the middle, there is usually a Latin ct underneath it — and usually an English word sitting on that ct: nocturnal, octave, fact, lactose, pectoral."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The strangest survivor is medical. To expectorate is to cough something up: ex-pectore, \"out of the chest.\" It is a very long word for a very short act, and it is built on this one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The chest, and a rule you can run in either direction."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ch in the middle of a Spanish word, ct in the Latin behind it, and an English word usually waiting on the far side."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el pecho",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el pecho*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el pecho y la espalda",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el pecho y la espalda*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el oído, then el pecho",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el oído*, then *el pecho*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does Latin ct become in Spanish? (Ch.) Which word for a time of day proves it? (La noche, from noctem.) And what does expectorate mean, taken apart? (Out of the chest.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C315-fuerza",
      "sequence": 4880,
      "title": "la fuerza — the strength inside comfort",
      "headword": "la fuerza",
      "romanization": "la fuerza",
      "gloss": "the strength — Latin fortia from fortis, with the o broken to ue; force, fort, effort and comfort are all built on it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:166b904612f6edeb",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does Latin ct turn into? (Ch.) And what is an audition? (A hearing.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el sueño — its o broke the same way."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enfermo — the state where this runs short."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "FUER-za, two beats. One slide through the middle, not two vowels. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la fuerza — \"the strength,\" \"the force.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin fortis meant strong. Its abstract noun fortia meant strength. Spanish broke the stressed o into ue, exactly as somnus became el sueño: fortia becomes fuerza."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the other crack, and now you have both of them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A stressed e goes to ie: piel, pierna, pie, diente. A stressed o goes to ue: sueño, fuerza. Two vowels, two breaks, and between them they account for a very large share of the Spanish dictionary."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has fortis lying in the open. A fort is a strong place. To fortify is to make strong. Force is fortia itself, arrived through French. An effort is strength put out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And then comfort. It is com-fortare: to strengthen thoroughly. To comfort somebody was not originally to soothe them — it was to put strength into them. The soft modern sense is a late arrival, and la fuerza is the word still sitting underneath it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both cracks, fully stated."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "E breaks to ie. O breaks to ue. Every word in these two chapters that could take one has taken it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la fuerza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la fuerza*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "no tengo fuerza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *no tengo fuerza*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el pecho, then la fuerza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el pecho*, then *la fuerza*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did fortis mean? (Strong.) What is comfort, taken apart? (To strengthen thoroughly.) And which break turned fortia into fuerza? (O to ue.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C315-descanso",
      "sequence": 4890,
      "title": "el descanso — the undoing of tiredness",
      "headword": "el descanso",
      "romanization": "el descanso",
      "gloss": "the rest — des- undoes cansar, to tire; rest in Spanish is defined as the reversal of being worn down",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:166cf055685ef571",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is comfort built on? (Fortis — strong.) And what does a Spanish ch usually sit on in Latin? (A ct.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la fuerza — what this gives back."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el sueño — a deeper version of the same thing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "des-CAN-so, three beats, stress in the middle. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el descanso — \"the rest,\" \"the break.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This one is assembled in front of you. Cansar is \"to tire.\" Des- undoes a verb. Descansar is \"to un-tire,\" and el descanso is the noun: the pause in which the un-tiring happens."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish does not name rest as a thing in its own right. It names it by what it reverses. Rest is the taking-back-off of tiredness."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cansar has a stranger history than it looks. It is Latin campsare, \"to turn about, to double back\" — a sailor's word for rounding a headland — and Latin had it from Greek kamptein, to bend. A ship that had rounded the cape had done the hard part of the voyage. From \"turned the corner\" to \"worn out by turning it\" is a short walk, and Spanish walked it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has no everyday descendant of kamptein, so there is no cognate to collect here. What there is instead is the seam. Des- is the same prefix as English dis-, so descanso is built exactly like disarm, disband and dislodge: a verb with its work run backwards."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word you could have assembled yourself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Des- is English dis-. Once you can see that seam, a large family of Spanish verbs comes apart in your hands."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el descanso",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el descanso*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un descanso, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un descanso, por favor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la fuerza, then el descanso",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la fuerza*, then *el descanso*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does des- do to a verb? (Undoes it.) What is cansar? (To tire.) And what was campsare, at sea? (To round a headland.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C315-miedo",
      "sequence": 4900,
      "title": "el miedo — the fear inside meticulous",
      "headword": "el miedo",
      "romanization": "el miedo",
      "gloss": "the fear — Latin metus, with the e broken to ie; meticulous once meant full of fear",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:606ef185d2f923d0",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does des- do? (Undoes the verb.) And what is an effort? (Strength put out.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tranquilo — its opposite."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "triste — its neighbour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "MIE-do, two beats. The d between vowels is soft, close to the th of this. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el miedo — \"the fear.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin metus, and the e breaks to ie once more. That is the fifth time. By now you should expect it before you are told."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has one descendant of metus and it is well hidden: meticulous. It is metus plus -culosus, \"full of fear.\" A meticulous person was, in Latin, a frightened one — somebody so afraid of getting it wrong that they checked everything twice, and then checked it again."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept the checking and let the fear go. Meticulous is now a compliment. But the dread is still down in the word, and el miedo is the part that got left behind."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It sits neatly among what you already have. Tranquilo is its opposite. Triste is its neighbour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The fifth ie, and the darker half of this chapter."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more feeling closes it out — and it is the opposite of this one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el miedo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el miedo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "tengo miedo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *tengo miedo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el descanso, then el miedo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el descanso*, then *el miedo*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did meticulous mean in Latin? (Full of fear.) What is the Latin behind miedo? (Metus.) And which of your five answers is its opposite? (Tranquilo.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C315-risa",
      "sequence": 4910,
      "title": "la risa — the laugh under ridicule",
      "headword": "la risa",
      "romanization": "la risa",
      "gloss": "the laughter — Latin risus from ridere, to laugh; English took the root only in unkind forms — ridicule, deride, risible",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:0287a542f4bae9a3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is meticulous, in Latin? (Full of fear.) And what does des- do to cansar? (Undoes the tiring.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "contento — the state this comes out of."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el miedo — what it is furthest from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "RI-sa, two beats. An r at the start of a word is rolled, not tapped. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la risa — \"the laughter.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin ridere was \"to laugh,\" and risus was the laugh itself. Spanish kept both halves cleanly: reír to laugh, la risa the laughter."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took the root, and took it badly. Every descendant it kept is unkind. To ridicule somebody is to laugh at them. To deride is de-ridere, to laugh down at them. Derision is the noun for doing it. Risible means laughable, and it has never once been a compliment."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So English has four words from ridere and not one of them is a happy laugh. Spanish has la risa, which is the ordinary, warm, unloaded word — the sound a room makes when something is funny."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is worth noticing about borrowing generally. A language rarely takes a whole word. It takes one use of the word, and the use it takes is very often the sharp one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ten of the body and what it feels."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And a hinge. The next chapter opens on the other side of this word — what the face does when the laughing stops."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la risa",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la risa*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la risa y el miedo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la risa y el miedo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el pecho, la fuerza, el descanso, el miedo, la risa",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el pecho, la fuerza, el descanso, el miedo, la risa*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was risus? (The laugh.) Name an English word from ridere. (Ridicule, deride, derision or risible.) And what do all of them have in common? (Not one is kind.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
